+Migration observations for `on`:
+
+```text
+first promotion: 2.092s, 451786 pages = 1764.8 MiB
+99% promotion:  3.113s, 905124 pages = 3535.6 MiB
+steady node0 anon: about 3.46 GiB
+steady node1 anon: about 12.54 GiB
+```
+
+This proves that the fixed 4G hot window is placement-friendly: the kernel moves
+the hot window to local memory quickly and without promotion failures.  But it
+is not strongly performance-friendly: after migration completes, throughput is
+only about 1.8 percent higher.
+
+## Why the 4G skewed-hotset workload is not strongly performance-friendly
+
+The workload initially looked like it should be strongly migration-friendly:
+
+- hot set is 4G, smaller than local capacity;
+- data starts on remote node1;
+- cgroup migration can promote the hot set to local node0;
+- worker threads run on node0 CPUs.
+
+The kernel did exactly that in the fixed-window run.  The problem is the
+microbenchmark's per-operation critical path.
+
+`mbench_run_skewed_hotset()` does not perform only one random memory load.  Each
+operation performs:
+
+- several `xorshift64` random-number steps;
+- modulo/divide operations for page and word selection;
+- branches for hot/cold and read/write/rmw selection;
+- address calculation;
+- then one 8-byte load or store.
+
+The binary disassembly confirmed that the read load is present, but the measured
+operation includes substantial CPU-side work.  With 32 worker threads, memory
+latency is also partially hidden by thread-level parallelism and memory-level
+parallelism.  As a result, remote-versus-local DRAM latency is not the dominant
+critical path.
+
+The observed throughput supports this:
+
+```text
+off fixed: 2.263B ops/s while all hot pages stayed remote
+on fixed:  2.303B ops/s after about 3.46 GiB was promoted local
+```
+
+That is only a 1-2 percent improvement, even though placement was correct.
+Therefore this workload is migration-friendly in the placement sense, but not
+in the performance sense.
+
+## Lessons learned
+
+1. Promotion success alone is not enough.
+
+   The kernel can migrate the expected pages, but the benchmark may still be
+   dominated by CPU work or parallelism that hides remote latency.
+
+2. The NUMA scanner depends on worker-thread lifetime.
+
+   Short-lived pthreads can suppress or delay hinting because scan progress is
+   tied to per-task runtime.  Persistent workers fixed the first-hint-fault
+   latency.
+
+3. Prefault placement must be separated from measurement.
+
+   Remote-first-touch plus a start gate is needed to avoid charging initial
+   placement to the measured phase.
+
+4. Moving hot windows create a moving-target problem.
+
+   With a 15s move interval, promotion catches the hot set quickly, but the
+   benchmark can pay migration cost repeatedly without seeing a large memory
+   locality benefit.
+
+5. A better friendly workload should make memory latency the critical path.
+
+   Candidate directions:
+
+   - precompute random indices outside the measured loop;
+   - reduce per-op RNG/modulo/branch cost;
+   - use dependent loads where the loaded value selects the next address;
+   - keep the hot set stable long enough for migration to amortize;
+   - avoid excessive thread-level parallelism if the goal is to expose latency.
+
