+# Adaptive-Migration VM Harness
+
+This directory contains a local QEMU/testing harness for the `Adaptive-Migration` kernel tree.
+
+What was copied from `/Serverless/Migration-friendly/scripts/kernel`:
+
+- `launch_kernel_qemu.sh`
+- the existing host/guest validation helpers
+- lightweight tenant workload helpers
+
+What was added for this repo:
+
+- `prepare_rootfs_overlay.sh`
+- `prepare_paper_workloads_overlay.sh`
+- `launch_paper_topology_qemu.sh`
+- `run_qemu_paper_cli_rss.sh`
+- `run_qemu_paper_cli_elapsed.sh`
+- `run_qemu_paper_cli_elapsed_sweep.sh`
+- `run_qemu_adaptive_migration_smoke.sh`
+- `validate_adaptive_migration_smoke.sh`
+
+Important defaults:
+
+- Kernel tree: this repo root
+- QEMU binary: `/Serverless/Migration-friendly/qemu/build/qemu-system-x86_64`
+- Base rootfs: `/Serverless/Migration-friendly/qemu/build/ubuntu.img`
+- Local overlay rootfs: `scripts/kernel/vm-images/adaptive-migration-base.qcow2`
+- Paper-workload overlay rootfs: `scripts/kernel/vm-images/adaptive-migration-paper-workloads.qcow2`
+
+The copied memcg validation scripts are kept as references, but they target the `Migration-friendly` memcg NUMA-knob kernel work, not the `Adaptive-Migration` paper branches directly.
+
+Recommended flow:
+
+1. Create or refresh the local rootfs overlay:
+
+```bash
+./scripts/kernel/prepare_rootfs_overlay.sh
+```
+
+To create a second overlay that already contains the paper workloads copied
+from `/Serverless/benchmark` plus the local surrogate microbenchmark:
+
+```bash
+./scripts/kernel/prepare_paper_workloads_overlay.sh
+```
+
+The prepared guest image installs the paper workload bundle under:
+
+```bash
+/Serverless/benchmark/paper-workloads
+```
+
+Inside the guest:
+
+```bash
+paper-workload --list
+paper-workload pr
+paper-workload liblinear-kdd12
+paper-workload silo
+paper-workload ft
+paper-workload lu
+paper-workload sp
+paper-workload gups-st
+paper-workload gups-mt
+paper-workload mbench-paper-micro
+paper-workload pair liblinear-ft 30
+```
+
+For the paper VM topology itself, use the dedicated launcher. It binds QEMU to
+all host node0 CPUs, backs guest node0 from host node0 memory, and backs guest
+node1 from host node2 memory:
+
+```bash
+./scripts/kernel/launch_paper_topology_qemu.sh \
+  --kernel-image ./arch/x86/boot/bzImage \
+  --local-dram 32G \
+  --remote-mem 128G
+```
+
+To run one workload command using the exact parameter set from
+`/Serverless/Migration-friendly/scripts/bench_V2/run_benchmark.py` and collect
+peak RSS inside the guest:
+
+```bash
+./scripts/kernel/run_qemu_paper_cli_rss.sh \
+  --kernel-image ./arch/x86/boot/bzImage \
+  --kernel-label ourwork \
+  --workload liblinear \
+  --local-dram 32G \
+  --timeout-sec 300
+```
+
+To run one workload with the same parameter set but collect elapsed time after
+applying the `ourwork` runtime settings inside the guest
+(`numa_balancing=2`, `demotion_enabled=1`, `demote_scale_factor=400`,
+`THP=never`, `SMT off`):
+
+```bash
+./scripts/kernel/run_qemu_paper_cli_elapsed.sh \
+  --kernel-image ./arch/x86/boot/bzImage \
+  --kernel-label ourwork \
+  --workload bc \
+  --local-dram 32G \
+  --timeout-sec 0
+```
+
+To sweep local DRAM sizes `16G,32G,48G` for one or more workloads:
+
+```bash
+./scripts/kernel/run_qemu_paper_cli_elapsed_sweep.sh \
+  --kernel-image ./arch/x86/boot/bzImage \
+  --kernel-label ourwork \
+  --workloads bc,pr
+```
+
+To reproduce the phase-candidate microbenchmark imported from the
+Migration-friendly workspace:
+
+```bash
+./scripts/kernel/run_qemu_phase_candidate_microbench.sh
+```
+
+The default phase-candidate settings match the latest 24 GiB sparse experiment:
+
+- VM: 32 vCPUs, 72 GiB memory
+- guest node0: 24 GiB on host node0, CPUs 0-31
+- guest node1: 48 GiB on host node2, CPU-less
+- QEMU memory backends: `policy=bind,prealloc=on`
+- cgroup local cap: `CAPACITY_PAGES=4194304` (16 GiB)
+- workload: `CANDIDATES=phase_tail_hotset_sparse24`
+- runtime: `ARENA_SIZE=48G`, `PHASE_MS=60000`, `PHASE_REPEAT=5`,
+  `LIVE_SAMPLE_SEC=5`, `SCAN_PERIOD_SCALE=1`, `HOT_THRESHOLD_MS=1`,
+  `NODE_BALANCING_ON=2`, `KSWAPD_DEMOTION_ON=1`
+
+The runner also accepts the policy list used during the latest adaptive
+experiments:
+
+```bash
+POLICIES=off,on,adaptive_global,adaptive_cgroup,oracle_cgroup_global0 \
+  ./scripts/kernel/run_qemu_phase_candidate_microbench.sh
+```
+
+Current caveat: the ICCD `refaultnearly-multitenant` kernel tree does not yet
+contain the Migration-friendly memcg NUMA knobs (`memory.node_capacity`,
+`memory.node_balancing`, `memory.kswapd_demotion_enabled`,
+`memory.numa_balancing_scan_period_scale`, and
+`memory.numa_balancing_hot_threshold_ms`). The phase runner is wired into this
+harness, but the cgroup-controlled policies require those kernel hooks before
+they can run successfully on the ICCD kernel.
+
+2. Check out the branch you want to test in this repo, or use a separate git worktree.
+
+Suggested paper-final branch:
+
+```bash
+git checkout refaultnearly-multitenant
+```
+
+3. Build the kernel image:
+
+```bash
+make -j"$(nproc)" bzImage
+```
+
+4. Optional: build a matching initramfs locally:
+
+```bash
+./scripts/kernel/launch_kernel_qemu.sh --build-initrd --dry-run
